# Coach speech (Slovak): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Slovak needs.
# Register: the coach says "ty" to the player (the game menus use the formal "vy"). The coach is male
# ("som rád", "zahral som"); the player's gender is unknown: no second-person past tense or
# conditional ("vyhral si", "mohol by si"), no adjective or participle agreeing with the player
# ("pripravený", "rád", "sám"): present or future tense, the neuter impersonal past ("podarilo sa ti",
# "stačilo vziať", "dalo sa vziať") and noun phrases instead.
# Words as in assets/i18n/sk.lang: pešiak, jazdec, strelec, veža, dáma, kráľ; šach, mat, pat, remíza,
# rošáda (malá / veľká), branie mimochodom, premena, ťah, pole, stĺpec, rad, partiár, hodiny, Elo.

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns: pešiak, jazdec, strelec, kráľ (masculine animate, gender "m": accusative = genitive, "vezmi
# môjho jazdca"), veža, dáma (feminine, gender "f"). owner.<owner>.<form> is the masculine pattern
# (also the default), owner.<owner>.<form>.f the feminine one. Every form below is defined for all six
# pieces and the four owners: your = tvoj, my = môj, and in the Analysis mode white = biely, black =
# čierny ("biely jazdec", "čiernu dámu"), with the same pronoun and agreement forms.
# Forms with the owner's word, the six cases:
#   nom    tvoj jazdec / tvoja veža          "{your} sa takto nepohybuje."
#   gen    tvojho jazdca / tvojej veže        "Môj kráľ stojí hneď vedľa {your:gen}."
#   dat    tvojmu jazdcovi / tvojej veži      "{my:dat} už nič nehrozí."
#   acc    tvojho jazdca / tvoju vežu         "Vezmi {my:acc}!"
#   loc    tvojom jazdcovi / tvojej veži      "Pri {my:loc} nestojí žiadny obranca."
#   ins    tvojím jazdcom / tvojou vežou       "S {your:ins} bol lepší ťah."
# "svoj" forms, when the subject of the clause owns the piece (Slovak never says "tvoj" / "môj" there):
#   refl_gen  svojho jazdca / svojej veže      "Mysli na bezpečnosť {your:refl_gen}."
#   refl_dat  svojmu kráľovi / svojej veži      "Daj {your:refl_dat} okienko."
#   refl_acc  svojho jazdca / svoju vežu        "Použi {your:refl_acc}.",
#                                              "Nechávam {my:refl_acc} bez ochrany."
#   refl_loc  svojom kráľovi / svojej dáme      "Premýšľaj o {your:refl_loc}."
#   refl_ins  svojím jazdcom / svojou vežou     "Potiahni {your:refl_ins}."
# The noun alone, no possessive (where "svoj" is heavy, or after an adjective of one's own):
#   bare_nom bare_gen bare_dat bare_acc bare_loc bare_ins   jazdec, jazdca, jazdcovi, jazdca, jazdcovi,
#   jazdcom: "Ťahaj {your:bare_ins}.", "Viazan{your:adj} {your:bare_nom} nekryje pole {sq}."
# Pronouns standing for the piece (masculine / feminine, the same for every owner):
#   pron       on / ona      the subject, only for emphasis (Slovak drops it): "{t1:pron} môže ustúpiť."
#   pron_gen   ho / jej      unstressed genitive: "Netreba sa {my:pron_gen} báť."
#   pron_dat   mu / jej      unstressed dative: "Nič {your:pron_dat} nehrozí."
#   pron_acc   ho / ju       unstressed object: "Vezmi {my:pron_acc}!" (second place in its clause)
#   spron_gen  jeho / jej    stressed genitive: "{my:spron_gen} sa neboj, sleduj {t1:acc}."
#   spron_dat  jemu / jej    stressed dative: "{t1:spron_dat} nič nehrozí, ale {t2:dat} áno."
#   spron_acc  jeho / ju     stressed object: "Vezmem {t2:spron_acc}, nie {t1:acc}."
#   npron_gen  neho / nej    after a preposition with the genitive: "Okolo {your:npron_gen} nie je ani
#                            jeden pešiak." (bez, do, od, z, u, okolo, vedľa)
#   npron_dat  nemu / nej    after k, proti: "Moje figúry sa blížia k {your:npron_dat}."
#   npron_acc  neho / ňu     after na, za, cez, pre: "Útočím na {your:npron_acc}."
#   npron_loc  ňom / nej     after na, v, o, pri, po: "Pri {my:npron_loc} nestojí žiadny obranca."
#   npron_ins  ním / ňou     after za, pred, s, nad, pod: "Za {your:npron_ins} stojí {t1}."
#   poss       jeho / jej    "its", of something the subject does not own (else "svoj"): "{your} je
#                            chyten{your:adj}: všetky {your:poss} únikové polia sú napadnuté."
# Agreement endings: the piece's gender in a word that agrees with it, glued to the stem (masculine /
# feminine). Put the piece placeholder itself first in the line: the coach points at the board on the
# first one.
#   adj        ý / á         "{your} je viazan{your:adj}." (viazaný / viazaná), "{t1}, ktor{t1:adj} ..."
#   adj_short  y / a         after a long syllable: "{your} musí byť aktívn{your:adj_short}."
#   adj_soft   í / ia        "{my} je lacnejš{my:adj_soft} ako {your}." (lacnejší / lacnejšia)
#   adj_gen    ého / ej      "{t1}, bez ktor{t1:adj_gen} ..."
#   adj_dat    ému / ej      "{t1}, ktor{t1:adj_dat} nič nehrozí"
#   adj_acc    ého / ú       "{reply} nechá {piece:acc} nekryt{piece:adj_acc}." (nekrytého / nekrytú)
#   adj_loc    om / ej       "{your}, o ktor{your:adj_loc} hovorím"
#   adj_ins    ým / ou       "{your}, za ktor{your:adj_ins} stojí {t1}"
#                            (the oblique endings are long: use them after a short syllable, as in
#                            ktor-, nekryt-, chránen-, viazan-)
#   past       - / a         past tense in -l: "{my} bol{my:past} nekryt{my:adj}." (bol / bola),
#                            "{your} mal{your:past} lepší ťah.", visel / visela, zostal / zostala
#   past_ol    ol / la       "{your} moh{your:past_ol} ísť na pole {sq}." (mohol / mohla), padol / padla
#   past_iel   iel / la      "{my} uš{my:past_iel}." (ušiel / ušla), odišiel / odišla, prišiel / prišla
# A piece as subject is never followed by a past tense, an adjective or a participle without one of
# these endings (the present tense needs none: "{my} napáda {your:acc}"). Two pieces together ("{t1} a
# {t2}") would need a plural depending on both: rephrase ("obe figúry sú napadnuté", "napádam {t1:acc}
# aj {t2:acc}").
# Prepositions s, z, k, v become so, zo, ku, vo before some words ("so strelcom", "ku kráľovi", "vo
# veži"): never put them before bare_* forms. Before the other forms they are fixed: "s {your:ins}",
# "k {my:dat}", "v {your:loc}", "z {my:gen}", but "so {your:refl_ins}", "zo {your:refl_gen}" (svoj-),
# and "s {my:npron_ins}", "k {my:npron_dat}", "z {my:npron_gen}" (ním, nemu, neho).
