# ---- Numbers -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Spoken number words; the plain word is the masculine inanimate nominative ("jeden ťah", "dva body").
# Slovak writes 21-99 and 101-999 as one word ("dvadsaťjeden", "stodvadsaťpäť"): the voice gets them
# as separate words ("dvadsať jeden", "sto dvadsať päť"), which it reads more reliably and which sound
# the same. Every hundred is listed. Forms ("{n:f}" looks up number.<n>.f.spoken first, a number
# without its own form says the plain word):
#   f     feminine 1-2: jedna, dve           ("jedna chyba", "dve figúry")
#   neut  neuter 1-2: jedno, dve             ("jedno pole", "dve percentá")
#   fa    feminine accusative 1: jednu       ("vzal som jednu figúru")
#   an    masculine animate 2-4: dvaja, traja, štyria   ("dvaja pešiaci", "traja útočníci")
#   ma    masculine animate accusative 1-4: jedného, dvoch, troch, štyroch   ("jedného pešiaka")
# Compounds ending in 1-4 (21, 32, 103 ...) never take these forms: the noun after them is in the
# genitive plural with the plain compound ("dvadsaťjeden ťahov", "dvadsaťdva chýb"), which is the
# plural rule's "other" form, where every count.* key below says the plain {n}.
